Stable stratification, buoyancy frequency and internal waves (@fd-stratified: 0, is #chapter, #part-of @fluid-dynamics, #source "../sources/Fluid-Mechanics-Tong.lingua#r_3SP698XHQFSZZ6N9Q22NESHMEA", #source "../sources/Advanced-Fluid-Dynamics-Mei-MIT.lingua#r_21NXVH78F0NYDPDVTG66PG261P", #source "../sources/NCFMF-Fluid-Mechanics-Films.lingua#r_0RT6J26HJJ83MX74030JPEHQVP", #fluid-dynamics "Boussinesq-flow-natural-convection-plumes-and-instability.lingua#r_3D346A9GVA8D8T39K4KHG0X3W0") { r_691GN8PDCYCW2BN2T3ETWSNC4B
**Read:** [Fluid Mechanics — David Tong](../sources/Fluid-Mechanics-Tong.lingua#r_3SP698XHQFSZZ6N9Q22NESHMEA), chapter 4 internal gravity waves and chapter 5 stratified instability; [Advanced fluid dynamics — Mei notes and MIT modules](../sources/Advanced-Fluid-Dynamics-Mei-MIT.lingua#r_21NXVH78F0NYDPDVTG66PG261P), stratified withdrawal, waves and thermal-flow notes; [Fluid mechanics films and illustrated notes — NCFMF](../sources/NCFMF-Fluid-Mechanics-Films.lingua#r_0RT6J26HJJ83MX74030JPEHQVP), stratified-flow demonstrations.

In a simple Boussinesq liquid with vertical background density ρ̄(z), small vertical displacements experience a buoyancy frequency satisfying N²=−(g/ρ_0)dρ̄/dz. Positive N² describes stable stratification under these assumptions. For compressible atmospheres, use the appropriate thermodynamic stratification variable instead of applying this incompressible formula indiscriminately.

Derive the displacement restoring force and then the linear internal-wave relation for an unbounded uniformly stratified nonrotating fluid. Distinguish frequency from the direction of phase and energy propagation. In the ideal two-dimensional case, ω²=N²k_h²/(k_h²+k_z²); rotation and boundaries change the permitted modes.

Explain how a density profile can suppress vertical transport while allowing horizontal motion and waves. Compare a velocity shear with stratification through a defined gradient Richardson number and the assumptions behind any stability criterion. Use measured temperature/composition and their density relation, including uncertainty, rather than treating color bands as quantitative density evidence.

**Continue:** [Boussinesq flow, natural convection, plumes and instability](Boussinesq-flow-natural-convection-plumes-and-instability.lingua#r_3D346A9GVA8D8T39K4KHG0X3W0).

